# mailbox-depth: `sink` loads with a mailbox depth of 2. `sender` sends it
# three messages in one Segment, so `sink` takes no turn in between: the
# first two wait in its mailbox, and the third finds it full and raises
# `mailbox full` at the `send` (sender:10, source 11:7). Nothing is sent, so
# no `send` record is written for it. The `catch` keeps its `to`, which is
# the receiver's name as text, since a Script isn't a value, and logs it.
# `sink` then handles the two that arrived, in order.
#
# The conformance minimum, 10000, isn't exercised directly: every message a
# Script sends is a `send` record, so filling a mailbox of 10000 would take
# over 20000 lines. This case pins the rule at a depth well inside it.
# Fuel, allocation and state figures are estimates.
# Unblessed: the output lines are written by hand, not by bless.

> load sender
> load sink

> deliver d1 to=sender message=flood
> pump clock=2026-09-28T09:00:00Z
send sender/r1 to=sink message=note args=[1]
send sender/r1 to=sink message=note args=[2]
raise sender/r1 code="mailbox full" at=sender:10 pos=11:7
call sender/r1.c1 op=log.write args=["full at 3"]
seg sender/r1 start delivery=d1 handler=flood clause=1 fuel=141 alloc=282 state=72 end=return
run sender/r1 outcome=completed delivery=d1 handler=flood fuel=141 alloc=282
# `sink` has its turns once `sender` has none left, loaded order first.
seg sink/r1 start from=sender/r1 handler=note clause=1 fuel=12 alloc=24 state=88 end=return
run sink/r1 outcome=completed handler=note fuel=12 alloc=24
seg sink/r2 start from=sender/r1 handler=note clause=1 fuel=12 alloc=24 state=64 end=return
run sink/r2 outcome=completed handler=note fuel=12 alloc=24
pumped state=idle fuel=165

> vars
vars sender fullTo="sink"
vars sink got=[1, 2]
